title: Whether a follower takes another's edit should mean little, and marks the author can see make it mean more.
pid: 0caa86e4-6a7f-4411-aeab-2f7238bc704e
body: |2-
  Caulfield: "I see your edits. What does it mean if I don't pull them
  back? You can't make it mean nothing ... But I would love it to mean
  less." In federated wiki's "happenings", a group writing together over
  hours, being forked or not felt like a verdict, and people felt they
  must keep up "for the good of the tribe"; over weeks it did not. His
  remedy for a page under heavy editing: write one's addition as a small
  page of its own and link it, since it stays visible however the big
  page is rewritten. Jeff's small notes are the same remedy.
  So my question whether processed marks should be graph data the
  author can see (in [[id:d3ed5d37-2a37-441a-b82b-03a39a89fdf2][reporting changes at any depth]]) has a cost
  on the side of visibility: at a fast tempo it turns reading into
  judging. For Jeff, who reads Claudes by their forks, the cost looks
  small; it is written here to be weighed, not to answer the question.
